1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,120
In this tutorial, we'll cover how to use projects in Burp Suite and various functions that are

2
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:20,160
useful when you're dealing with project files. Burp projects let you organize your work on a

3
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:27,680
specific test. When you launch Burp, it shows this wizard on startup. And here you can choose

4
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:35,120
what kind of project to open. You can open a temporary project. This is ideal for quick tasks.

5
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:41,680
All the data is held in memory, nothing is saved by default, and your work will be lost when Burp

6
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:49,680
exits. You can choose to create a new project on disk. This means that all your data is saved in a

7
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:57,360
project file. It's saved incrementally in real time as you work. And also your memory usage

8
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:01,120
when you're using Burp will be lower because a lot of data is pushed onto the disk.

9
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:05,840
Finally, if you've created a project on disk previously, you can open it.

10
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:09,680
So let's create a new project and give it a name.

11
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:15,280
We need to select where we're going to save the project.

12
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:21,120
And when we do that, if we want, Burp will just give us a default file name to save it to.

13
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:26,720
If we click next, this is where we choose the configuration options

14
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:30,720
to use for the project. We'll explain these in a different tutorial.

15
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:33,440
So click start Burp.

16
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,080
And Burp opens with the project we created.

17
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:43,840
At this point, as normal, we would do some work and create some data. So I'm just going to create

18
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:45,600
a quick new scan.

19
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:56,800
And I'm going to do just a bit of lightweight browsing using Burp's browser.

20
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:10,160
So now we can see we've got some data in the proxy history and we have a scan running on the dashboard.

21
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:13,600
So at this point, I'm just going to close Burp.

22
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,920
Here we launch Burp again. We have the same startup wizard.

23
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:27,280
And now we can see that the project that I previously created is listed here for us to select.

24
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,520
So I'm going to select to open that project.

25
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:37,440
One thing worth noting is that automated tasks are paused by default when you open an existing project.

26
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:43,120
So this will affect live tasks, scans, and anything else that's running in the task list in the dashboard.

27
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:50,160
I'm going to select the option to use the options that were saved with the project.

28
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:53,840
All project options are automatically saved within the project file.

29
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:58,640
So anything we've customized as we were working will be preserved when we reopen the project.

30
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:01,360
So click start Burp.

31
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:04,160
And the project I selected will open.

32
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:09,200
The project opens pretty much instantly, the same time it took to create a new project.

33
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:14,640
If you did have a huge amount of work saved in your project file, it might take a little bit longer to open.

34
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:20,000
So here we can see task execution is paused because that's the default behavior.

35
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,440
So we can see the scan that I created is sitting there where it left off.

36
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:31,040
We can see the data that we accumulated in the proxy history is still there.

37
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:34,160
And other data that we accumulated as we were working is still there.

38
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:38,480
At this point, I can now resume task execution.

39
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:42,960
And that means the scan that was in progress will resume as before.

40
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:49,680
It's worth mentioning that if you reopen a project file where you previously did some vulnerability scanning,

41
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:56,640
when you reopen the file, Burp will resume collaborator polling and will sometimes report some vulnerabilities

42
00:03:56,640 --> 00:04:00,400
retrospectively which were triggered after the end of your previous scanning.

43
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:05,440
There are some other functions which are useful when you're working with project files.

44
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:11,360
If you create a temporary project, you can save a copy of it from the project menu.

45
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:16,480
And you can do the same thing if you've made an on-disc project and you want a second copy of the project file.

46
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:23,920
In both cases, you can choose which Burp tools to include data from and whether to only include

47
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:25,440
in scope items.

48
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,240
This can be really useful to reduce the size of a large project file.

49
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:34,400
You can also import a project file.

50
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:39,120
This is really useful if you want to merge your work together from something you produced on another occasion.

51
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:46,000
And also Burp creates backup copies of your project files automatically by default.

52
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:51,760
You can configure this if you go to User Options, MISC, Automatic Project Backup.

53
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:56,720
Here you can configure how frequently the backup files are created and some other options.

54
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:04,320
So that's how to use Burp Suite projects and various functions that are useful when working with project files.

